# Whether an object the build cache would serve is the object its compile
# wrote (design.md §1.12, §3.1; defect 443, 2026-10-07).
#
# **An object a power cut left as zeros was served by every later build.**
# Measured on the base, this Mac: the runtime's object or a module's, zeroed
# at its length, and every build after it said `ld: unknown file type`, then
# *clang refused the generated C*, at exit 2, until `build/` was deleted by
# hand. Defect 357 had made the bytes durable before the name
# (`publish.publish` flushes before its rename) and sealed every kept TEXT
# (`cli/kept.hero`); an object was served on its record and its existence
# alone, so a file a disk lost, a `build/` written before that repair, or
# one cut or written over by anything else stood in for the object, and the
# link was the first to look at its bytes.
#
# **So each object carries a seal, beside it, of its own bytes**: the key
# `module/reading.key_of` gives a file, the one the headers a compile read are
# keyed by, which is a function of every byte, a binary's included. The seal
# is taken of what clang wrote, before the object is published, and is a kept
# text (`cli/kept.hero`), so a seal that is not whole is not read either.
# Before an object is served its bytes are keyed again and held to the seal:
# zeros, a cut, a block lost in the middle, another file at the name, each
# moves the key, and the object is compiled again and the build says so. A
# check of the header alone would pass an object whose header survived and
# whose body did not, which is what a power cut can leave of a large file.
#
# **An object with no seal at all is compiled again in silence**: that is a
# `build/` an older compiler left, whose objects were never sealed, and not a
# fault; it costs one cold compile per object, once.
#
# **The cost is a read and a key of every object a build serves**, measured
# 2026-10-07 in instructions retired: 1,158 a byte of object at -O0 over this
# compiler's 472 objects, 178 of them the runtime's shown read, which writes
# each byte that is not UTF-8 as three, and the rest `cli/digest`'s loop. A
# warm build of `print(1)` +13.4% (three objects), of `examples/interpreter`
# +14.6%, of this compiler +4.0% (32 MB of objects). A check of the header and
# the length would cost almost nothing, and pass a block zeroed in the middle,
# which on the base linked and ran.
#
# **Since defect 484 the key is made in C over the bytes** (`key_of`,
# `runtime/parts/key.c`), the same sixteen characters, and the text the
# Heroes route built for it is not built. Measured 2026-10-09 on this Mac,
# instructions retired by a warm build against the compiler before it:
# `print(1)` 2.53e9 to 0.55e9, `examples/interpreter` 8.76e9 to 5.99e9, this
# compiler 418e9 to 379e9: the key was not the seal's alone, every header and
# runtime file a build reads is keyed by it too.

use cli/io
use cli/kept
use cli/process
use cli/publish
use module/reading as file_reading

# What a served object's bytes say against its seal.
variant Checked
    # The object its compile wrote, byte for byte.
    whole
    # No seal stands beside it: a `build/` no seal was written in.
    unsealed
    # A seal stands beside it and the bytes are not what it holds, or the
    # seal itself is not whole: something changed the file after its compile.
    changed

# The seal's name, beside the object.
function seal_of(object: str) -> str
    return object + ".key.txt"

# Seal the object `staged` holds, which is about to be published as `object`:
# the key of the bytes clang wrote. Where it cannot be sealed, the staged file
# is discarded with it, since it would be published with nothing to vouch.
function seal(staged: str, object: str) -> ()?
    key = file_reading.key_of(staged)
    sealed: ()? @= ok()

    if key.is_err()
        sealed @ fail(code: "failed", msg: "cannot read the object clang wrote at " + staged)
    else
        sealed @ kept.write(path: seal_of(object), text: key.must())

    if sealed.is_err()
        publish.discard(staged)
    return sealed

# Whether the cache may serve `object`: it is there and it is whole. One that
# changed is said before the caller compiles it again; one with no seal is
# compiled again in silence.
function serves(object: str) -> bool
    if !process.path_exists(object)
        return false
    checked = check(object)

    if checked == .changed
        io.eprint(text: told(object))
    return checked == .whole

# The object's bytes held to its seal.
function check(object: str) -> Checked
    if !process.path_exists(seal_of(object))
        return .unsealed
    sealed = kept.read(seal_of(object))

    if sealed.is_err()
        return .changed
    found = file_reading.key_of(object)

    if found.is_err() || found.must() != sealed.must()
        return .changed
    return .whole

# What the build says of an object that is not the one its compile wrote,
# before it compiles it again.
function told(object: str) -> str
    return "the cached object " + object + " is not the one its compile wrote: its bytes changed after it was written, as a power cut or a failing disk can leave a file, so it is compiled again"
